  "carePelvicScienceTitle": "Pelvic floor",
  "carePelvicScienceBadge": "Physiotherapy",
  "carePelvicScienceQuick": "The pelvic floor muscles support the bladder, the bowel and, in women, the uterus. Training them helps control leaks, recover after childbirth or prostate surgery, and improves sexual function, for any sex and at any age.",
  "carePelvicScienceHow": "Each session has three parts: slow squeezes (strength and endurance), quick squeezes (for coughing, sneezing or laughing) and a final relaxation that matters just as much, because a muscle that only squeezes gets tense. Always rest at least as long as you squeeze. To find them, imagine holding in both pee and wind at once; don't squeeze your buttocks, thighs or tummy and don't hold your breath.",
  "carePelvicScienceAdvice": "With one to three sessions a day, most people notice changes in 8 to 12 weeks. If you feel pain, discomfort when squeezing, or can't let go, use “Just relax” and see a pelvic floor physiotherapist. Don't practise by stopping your flow while peeing.",
  "carePelvicScienceRefs": "Dumoulin C et al. (2018). Pelvic floor muscle training versus no treatment for urinary incontinence in women. Cochrane.\nNICE NG123 (2019). Urinary incontinence and pelvic organ prolapse in women.\nAnderson CA et al. (2015). Conservative management for postprostatectomy urinary incontinence. Cochrane.",
