  "careBreaksScienceTitle": "Active breaks and eye rest",
  "careBreaksScienceBadge": "Ergonomics",
  "careBreaksScienceQuick": "Sitting still for a long time is linked to neck and back pain, poorer circulation and more tiredness. Short, frequent breaks ease those aches more than one long session at the end of the day.",
  "careBreaksScienceHow": "Each routine lasts 1 to 3 minutes and is done in your chair or standing. Hold stretches without bouncing and without reaching pain. Eye rest follows the 20-20-20 rule: every 20 minutes of screen time, look at something 20 feet (about six metres) away for 20 seconds to relax your focus; blinking slowly restores your tear film.",
  "careBreaksScienceAdvice": "A break every hour or two is enough. If a movement hurts, skip it. You can turn on the eye rest reminder in Settings → Reminders.",
  "careBreaksScienceRefs": "Waongenngarm P et al. (2018). Effects of active break and postural shift interventions on recovery from prolonged sitting. Applied Ergonomics.\nAmerican Academy of Ophthalmology (2021). Computers, digital devices and eye strain.\nWHO (2020). Guidelines on physical activity and sedentary behaviour.",
