  "careVitalsScienceTitle": "How to read your vital signs",
  "careVitalsScienceBadge": "Reference",
  "careVitalsScienceQuick": "A single reading says little: what's useful is the trend, always measured in similar conditions.",
  "careVitalsScienceHow": "Blood pressure (AHA 2017): normal below 120/80; elevated between 120 and 129 with the lower number below 80; high from 130 or from 80. Measure it seated, after five minutes' rest, with your arm supported at heart level.\nGlucose (ADA): fasting, normal from 70 to 99 mg/dL; 100 to 125, above; from 126, high. Two hours after eating, normal below 140.\nOxygen: normal from 95 %; below 92 % it's worth seeing a doctor.\nTemperature: normal from 35.5 to 37.2 °C; low-grade fever from 37.3 to 37.9; fever from 38.",
  "careVitalsScienceAdvice": "If a reading is very high or very low and you feel unwell (chest pain, shortness of breath, confusion, fainting), get urgent help: call your local emergency number. Take the PDF report to your appointment so your doctor can see the trend.",
  "careVitalsScienceRefs": "Whelton PK et al. (2018). 2017 ACC/AHA Guideline for High Blood Pressure in Adults. Hypertension.\nAmerican Diabetes Association (2024). Standards of Care in Diabetes.\nWHO (2011). Pulse Oximetry Training Manual.",
