  "sciElectrolitoExtracelularUl2300": "Extracellular electrolyte • UL: 2,300 mg/day",
  "sciElSodioRegulaElVolumen": "Sodium regulates blood volume, osmotic pressure and nerve-muscle signalling.",
  "sciConsumirMasDe2300": "Eating more than 2,300 mg of sodium a day stimulates vasopressin (ADH) and holds on to extracellular water. Each extra gram of salt can hold up to 200 ml of water, causing +1 to +2 kg jumps on the scale that aren't fat gain.",
  "sciUlSodio2300Mg": "Sodium UL = 2,300 mg/day (≈ 5.75 g of table salt)",
  "sciSiTuPesoSubeTras": "If your weight goes up after a salty meal, don't cut calories drastically. Drink more water and eat more potassium to restore the balance.",
  "sciPotasioKYBalanceCelular": "Potassium (K) and cell balance",
  "sciElectrolitoIntracelularMeta3500": "Intracellular electrolyte • Goal: 3,500 - 4,700 mg",
  "sciElPotasioContrarrestaLosEfectos": "Potassium counteracts sodium, relaxes blood vessels and helps build glycogen.",
  "sciLaBombaNaKAtpasa": "The Na+/K+ ATPase pump needs a K:Na ratio above 2:1. In whole foods there's no toxic ceiling for healthy kidneys; in concentrated supplements the safe limit is 4,700 mg.",
  "sciRelacionKNaOptima2": "Optimal K:Na ratio ≥ 2.0 : 1",
  "sciNasemDietaryReferenceIntakesFor": "NASEM. Dietary Reference Intakes for Sodium and Potassium.",
  "sciPriorizaFrutasVerdurasAguacatesY": "Put fruit, vegetables, avocados and potatoes first. Potassium from food speeds up the removal of excess sodium.",
  "sciMagnesioMgYToleranciaSuplementaria": "Magnesium (Mg) and supplement tolerance",
  "sciCofactorEnzimaticoUlSuplementario350": "Enzyme cofactor • Supplement UL: 350 mg",
  "sciEsencialParaProduccionDeAtp": "Essential for producing ATP, building protein, muscle contraction and sleep quality.",
  "sciElMagnesioDieteticoNoTiene": "Magnesium from food isn't toxic, but supplements (oxide, citrate) have a Tolerable Upper Limit (UL) of 350 mg/day because of their osmotic effect, which can cause diarrhoea if exceeded.",
  "sciUlSuplementario350MgDia": "Supplement UL = 350 mg/day of elemental magnesium",
  "sciInstituteOfMedicineUsDietary": "Institute of Medicine (US). Dietary Reference Intakes for Magnesium.",
  "sciTomaSuplementosDeMagnesioBisglicinato": "Take magnesium supplements (bisglycinate or citrate) with dinner to help your nervous system relax.",
  "sciFibraDieteticaYMicrobiota": "Dietary fibre and gut microbiota",
  "sciSaludIntestinalYSaciedadMeta": "Gut health and fullness • Goal: 25 - 38 g/day",
  "sciLaFibraDieteticaModulaLa": "Dietary fibre slows glucose absorption, improves fullness and feeds your gut bacteria with short-chain fatty acids.",
  "sciLaFibraSolubleRetrasaEl": "Soluble fibre slows stomach emptying and keeps you full for longer, while insoluble fibre speeds up transit through the colon. Bacteria ferment fibre into butyrate and propionate.",
  "sciMetaDiaria14GDe": "Daily goal: 14 g of fibre per 1,000 kcal eaten",
  "sciAumentaLaFibraDeManera": "Increase fibre gradually and always with plenty of water for smooth digestion.",
  "sciGastoEnergeticoDiarioTdee": "Total daily energy expenditure (TDEE)",
  "sciMetabolismoBasalActividad": "Basal metabolism + activity",
  "sciTuTdeeEsLaEnergia": "Your TDEE is the energy your body really uses in 24 hours: basal metabolism, digestion and movement.",
  "sciADiferenciaDeLasFormulas": "Unlike standard static formulas that assume fixed theoretical factors, NB SelfCare uses a 14-day exponential smoothing filter (EMA), comparing your daily calorie intake with the real change in your smoothed weight, to work out your true energy burn.",
  "sciTdeeIngestaDiariaKcalPeso": "TDEE = Daily intake (kcal) - [Δ Smoothed weight (kg) × 7700 kcal / Days]",
  "sciRegistraTusComidasYPesate": "Log your meals and weigh yourself often. The more consecutive days with records, the more precise the calibration.",
  "sciTendenciaDePesoYFluctuaciones": "Weight trend and fluctuations",
  "sciMasaGrasaVsAguaTransitoria": "Fat mass vs temporary water",
  "sciLasSubidasOBajadasBruscas": "Sudden day-to-day jumps or drops in weight are almost never body fat, but changes in water, glycogen and gut contents.",
  "sciAlmacenar1GramoDeGlucogeno": "Storing 1 gram of glycogen holds 3 to 4 grams of water in your cells. A meal with more carbs or sodium, a hard workout, taking creatine or the luteal phase can shift your weight by 1 to 2.5 kg of temporary water.",
  "sci1gGlucogeno35gH2o": "1g glycogen = +3.5g H₂O held in cells",
  "sciNoTeAlarmesPorPicos": "Don't be alarmed by one-off spikes. Always look at the smoothed weekly moving average (EMA) and the body fat measured by impedance.",
  "sciDecaimientoDeLaCafeina": "Caffeine decay",
  "sciCronobiologiaSuenoN3": "Chronobiology and deep sleep",
  "sciLaCafeinaTieneUnaSemivida": "Caffeine has an average half-life of {halfLifeHours} hours. Even when you no longer feel it, the remaining molecules block adenosine receptors and reduce restorative deep sleep.",
  "sciLaAdenosinaEsElNeurotransmisor": "Adenosine is the neurotransmitter that builds up sleep pressure. Caffeine competes with it at the A1 and A2A receptors. If you go to bed with more than 25 mg active, slow-wave deep sleep (N3) gets fragmented.",
  "sciTuHoraDeCorteAconsejada": "Your recommended cut-off for coffee or pre-workout is {bedtimeCurfewTime}. Keeping the residual level under 25 mg at bedtime (projected today at {p1} mg) restores your physical and mental recovery.",
  "sciSistemaDeClasificacionNova": "The NOVA classification",
  "sciProcesamientoAlimentario": "Food processing",
  "sciElSistemaNovaClasificaLos": "NOVA classifies foods not by their individual nutrients but by how much, and why, they've been industrially processed (Groups 1 to 4).",
  "sciGrupo1AlimentosSinProcesar": "• Group 1: Unprocessed or minimally processed foods (fruit, vegetables, eggs, fresh meat, pulses).\n• Group 2: Processed culinary ingredients (olive oil, butter, salt, spices).\n• Group 3: Processed foods (simple preserves, aged cheeses, artisan bread).\n• Group 4: Ultra-processed products (industrial formulations with emulsifiers, colourings, flavourings and refined flours).",
  "sciApuntaAQueMasDel": "Aim for more than 70–80% of your calories to come from Groups 1 to 3. The key isn't perfection, but building a solid base of real food with minimal industrial processing.",
